In conclusion, the long-term photostability evaluation as powders of BDC-containing MOFs shows that, in contrast to the numerous reports in the field, they decompose evolving ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ in a significant percentage respect to the initial carboxylate content. The temporal $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ evolution depends on the composition and structure of the MOF, being markedly high for MIL101 Fe, UiO66 Zr or MIL125 Ti- $\cdot N H_{2}$ , which are currently among the most used MOF in photocatalysis. The differences in photodecarboxylation rate reflects the influence of the node-linker interaction on the deactivation of the exciton localized on the linker. Interestingly, in spite of the large decarboxylation level that can be achieved, routine characterization techniques such as XRD, UV-Vis spectroscopy and TGA fail to report on this photochemical reaction, while adsorption measurements reveal changes that are difficult to correlate directly with the level of decarboxylation. Solid-state $^{13}\mathsf{C}$ NMR spectroscopy is a suitable technique when the degree of photodecarboxylation is sufficiently high. Overall, the present experimental data shows that the widely assumed photostability tests for MOFs have to be revisited by conducting studies at long irradiation times and measuring gas evolution.
